[THOUGHT] The patient has known Crohn disease and now presents with acute-onset gait ataxia ("drunk-like" walking), which strongly suggests cerebellar or vestibular dysfunction. Given the underlying inflammatory bowel disease, key differential considerations include:  
- Nutritional deficiencies (e.g., B12, thiamine, vitamin E) due to malabsorption;  
- Paraneoplastic or autoimmune neurologic syndromes (e.g., anti-Yo, anti-Hu, anti-GAD65, or anti-NMDA receptor);  
- CNS infection or inflammation (e.g., PML in immunosuppressed patients);  
- Structural lesion (e.g., posterior fossa mass, stroke);  
- Toxic/metabolic causes (e.g., medication side effect, alcohol, heavy metals).  

First, I need to assess for red-flag neurological signs and determine acuity and progression. A focused neurological examination — especially coordination, gait, and nystagmus — is essential before ordering labs or imaging. Since gait ataxia is the dominant symptom, cerebellar exam (e.g., finger-to-nose, heel-to-shin, Romberg, tandem gait) and assessment of nystagmus are top priorities.

[TEST] Neurological Examination - Coordination

